What would you expect, as a user that has just read the documentation?

> ... I would expect that the latter would always be chosen, because that's the preference I indicated, and it has a signature implementation with the name I'm asking for. That's what existing code does, isn't it?

That is not my interpretation of preference at all. According to the man page the "?" operator has this effect:

> ? is a prefix operator that means that the following clause is optional but preferred.

So this definitely does *not* mean that we *always* apply it. It is explicitly optional. As to "prefer", google defines the word as:

> like (one thing or person) better than another or others; tend to choose.

So, I expect that in the case where all other things being equal we should choose the preferred one. But we are not *required* to do so if things are not equal. In the case where we know that choosing an option from a different provider will definitely fail then this seems like exactly the case where we should not follow the preference.

> What I'm hearing is that if the given key lives in a different provider and if it can't exported, then we must get a signature from the same provider as the key instead of what the caller asked to have fetched (given the choices above). Have I finally been able to express the problem you're talking about correctly?

There is also the case to consider where no preference has been expressed, but multiple providers match the property query. For example consider this variant of my earlier example:

````
/* Load the key from "myprovider". Key is unexportable. */
EVP_PKEY *pkey = load_my_pkey("provider=myprovider");

/* No explicit provider is set. Either "default" or "myprovider" match the property query */
EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey(NULL, pkey, NULL);

EVP_PKEY_sign_init(pctx);
````

In this case the property query is NULL, so either "default" or "myprovider" *could* be returned (which is chosen is undefined). However we know one works and one doesn't. In my mind at least we should always return the one that works and not one that doesn't.
